      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5ba749f34dca6b351d658330|full_name}: But they haven't given up efforts to support Quest? It wouldn't surprise me if there are more active Quest Standalone players than PCVR and Desktop combined. The hardware is simply too limited and the current limits set are in my opinion, completely appropriate.\nYes, the Quest could handle more things in a controlled environment. Better avatar shaders, larger avatar file size, more supported Unity components etc. But there is no viable way to scale the things you see, and the fidelity of avatars shown based on things like player count and world optimisation. The current solution of keeping things very limited is the best solution. Because it just works 99% of the time in a user friendly way.\nYou need to remember the Quest Standalone platform is not for enthusiasts. I would argue that PC is. The Quest is essentially a console and it needs to offer a good experience out of the box. Most Quest VRC players will not understand how to optimise the game by changing settings, limiting who they see and so on. VRC needs to be a very consistent experience on Quest, which is something they don't have to worry about nearly as much on PC because of its much higher specification potential. We can scale our hardware up to meet any demand.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
